Worked example:

```javascript
function orderedNotes(notes) {
  return [...notes].sort((a,b)=>a.created-b.created || a.id-b.id);
}
console.log(orderedNotes([{"id":2,"created":10},{"id":1,"created":10}]));
```
The returned value is [{"id":1,"created":10},{"id":2,"created":10}].

after:
```javascript
function orderedNotes(notes) {
  return [...notes].sort((a,b)=>a.created-b.created || a.id-b.id);
}
console.log(orderedNotes([{"id":2,"created":10},{"id":1,"created":10}]));
```
Both notes have created: 10. The result puts ID 1 before ID 2 because their creation values tie.
